      "point": "To mitigate the risks of default and improve project viability, legislative proposals focus on two key amendments: 1) Implementing a revised loan program to grant greater flexibility and allow for executive action (waiving/decreasing interest charges) via Amendment One to S1. 2) Amending King County Code to update the minimum bed capacity threshold from fifty (50) to sixty (60) to accommodate financially feasible projects, thereby addressing historical limitations.",
      "speaker": [
        "Rod Domowsk",
        "Executive staff"
      ],
      "action_owner": []
    },
    {
      "point": "Long-term financial sustainability for C H S and the Aurora Oaks Enhanced Shelter hinges on securing public funding. While T O D grants ($10 million available in 2025) are a potential solution, repayment strategy relies heavily on successfully applying to this fund after navigating earlier difficulties with insufficient funding phases (only five of eight needed). The ultimate feasibility depends on matching financial forecasts and the Executive's ability to secure necessary grant dollars.",
      "speaker": [
        "C H S representative",
        "Rod Domowsk"
      ],
      "action_owner": []
    },
    {
      "point": "The ongoing operational use of a former senior care facility as a 62-bed shelter for Lake City Partners is supported by legislative focus on maintaining existing capacity while pursuing redevelopment into permanent supportive housing. Future interest payment pressures (potentially exceeding $200,000 annually) pose an existential risk to the current operation unless Amendment One allows for the Executive to waive or decrease these charges.",
      "speaker": [
